Findings (efficiency angle):

```json
[
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/api/verification/lean/route.ts",
    "line": 34,
    "summary": "The 35s abort timer created at line 34 is only cleared on the success/non-OK paths (line 43). If `fetch` rejects (ECONNREFUSED, DNS failure, TLS error) control jumps to the catch at line 53, which returns `unavailableResponse(\"verifier-unreachable\")` without `clearTimeout(timeout)`. Cheaper alternative: wrap in `try { ... } finally { clearTimeout(timeout); }` (hoist `timeout` above the try), or use `AbortSignal.timeout(REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS)` and drop the manual timer entirely.",
    "failure_scenario": "The verifier host is down, so every POST fails fast (~1ms) but leaves a live 35s timer plus its AbortController retained on the heap. During an auto-formalize run over 20 nodes, 20 timers pile up; in a Node/serverless runtime the pending timers keep the event loop non-empty, so the function instance cannot freeze/exit for up to 35s after the response was already sent — billed idle time per failed request, and the abort fires on an already-settled controller."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/formalization/api.ts",
    "line": 120,
    "summary": "`verifyLean` has no memory of a verifier that just reported `unavailable`. The new `unavailable` flag is consumed per call site and discarded, so every subsequent verify in the same session pays a full round trip to `/api/verification/lean`, which (for `verifier-unreachable`) may block on the 35s fetch in route.ts. Cheaper alternative: a module-level circuit breaker in api.ts — cache `{unavailable: true, reason}` with a short TTL (e.g. 30-60s, reset on user-initiated Re-verify) and return it synchronously without the fetch; optionally mirror it server-side so the outbound fetch is skipped too.",
    "failure_scenario": "LEAN_VERIFIER_URL points at a host that black-holes packets (hung TCP connect rather than refused). Auto-formalizing a 15-node decomposition performs 15 independent verify calls, each hitting the full 35s timeout — roughly 8.75 minutes of pure dead wait added to the run, on top of the same information already learned from node 1."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/hooks/useAutoFormalizeQueue.ts",
    "line": 128,
    "summary": "`formalizeNode` collapses the new `unavailable` outcome into `\"failed\"` (formalizeNode.ts:61 — `result.valid ? \"verified\" : \"failed\"`), so the queue at lines 132-139 adds the node to `failedIds`, and every dependent node then hits the `hasFailedDep` branch at line 113 and is marked failed/skipped without ever being generated. Cheaper alternative: return a third `\"unavailable\"` outcome from `formalizeNode`, keep those nodes out of `failedIds` (a missing verifier says nothing about the dependency's Lean code), and either surface a \"verifier offline\" queue status or let the run finish generation with verification deferred.",
    "failure_scenario": "The verifier is offline for the duration of one run over a 20-node DAG. The 3 roots are generated then marked failed; the 17 dependents are skipped with \"Skipped: dependency X failed\" and produce no semiformal/Lean at all. When the verifier comes back the user must re-run the whole queue, so the 3 roots' semiformal+Lean LLM calls are paid twice and the other 17 nodes' work was pointlessly deferred rather than done while the model calls were already in flight."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/formalization/leanRetryLoop.ts",
    "line": 71,
    "summary": "The unavailable check is correctly placed to stop the retry loop, but it still sits after a full `generateLean`/`generateLeanStreaming` call (lines 59-61), and nothing upstream can short-circuit because the loop never consults a cached verifier state before generating. Cheaper alternative: consult the circuit-breaker suggested for api.ts:120 before entering the loop — when the verifier is already known unavailable, generate once and return `{unavailable: true}` without issuing the verify request at all (currently one wasted request per node), and skip the `dependencyContext + currentCode` concatenation at line 70 that is built solely to be discarded.",
    "failure_scenario": "Per node in a queue run against a down verifier: one HTTP POST carrying the full dependency context plus generated Lean source (often tens of KB) is uploaded to a route that, in the `verifier-not-configured` case, discards `leanCode` at route.ts:26 without reading it — N pointless request bodies serialized, uploaded, and JSON-parsed server-side for a constant answer."
  }
]
```

Not flagged (checked, judged trivial): `LeanCodeDisplay.tsx:132` adds only a static JSX banner and one extra `===` comparison at line 111; `VerificationBadge.tsx:11` adds a single branch — neither introduces per-render allocation or work worth changing. The duplicated `onSessionUpdate` calls on the unavailable path in `useFormalizationPipeline.ts:125-126` are coalesced by the debounced localStorage write in `useFormalizationSessions.ts:44-57`, so they cost no extra I/O.

