Findings:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/formalization/formalizeNode.ts",
    "line": 61,
    "summary": "The per-node formalization pipeline reads `result.valid` directly (`deductiveResult = result.valid ? \"verified\" : \"failed\"`) and never consults the new `unavailable` flag on LeanRetryResult. Because leanRetryLoop's unavailable early-return sets `{ valid: false, errors: \"\" }`, an offline verifier marks the node \"failed\". This is the exact consumer the two-boolean design invites to misread; `verifyResultToStatus` exists in api.ts but this call site bypasses it.",
    "failure_scenario": "With LEAN_VERIFIER_URL unset (the Vercel case this PR targets), auto-formalizing a decomposition turns every node red \"Failed\" with an empty error body — the opposite of the PR's stated goal of not letting verifier absence be read as a proof verdict. Downstream code compounds it: useAutoFormalizeQueue.ts:106/117 and usePanelDefinitions.tsx:57 (`verifiedCount`) and leanContext.ts:43 all key off \"verified\"/\"failed\", so unchecked proofs are permanently excluded from dependency context and counted as failures."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/types/decomposition.ts",
    "line": 32,
    "summary": "`toNodeVerificationStatus` / `fromNodeVerificationStatus` are the shared global↔node status bridge and both use a `default:` arm, so the new \"unavailable\" silently collapses to \"unverified\" (and round-trips back as \"none\"). NodeVerificationStatus was not extended. page.tsx:350 pushes pipeline status through this mapping for the node pipeline, and useActiveArtifactState.ts:41-42 maps it back to feed LeanCodeDisplay/VerificationBadge.",
    "failure_scenario": "In decomposition mode (a node selected), the new amber \"Verifier offline — proof not checked\" banner and the Re-verify button added to LeanCodeDisplay can never render: the status is erased at page.tsx:350 and comes back as \"none\" through useActiveArtifactState. The feature works only on the global workspace path, and the gap is invisible because `default:` suppresses the exhaustiveness error that would otherwise flag it."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/formalization/api.ts",
    "line": 104,
    "summary": "VerifyLeanResult models the outcome as two independent booleans (`valid`, `unavailable`) whose combination only means something after passing through `verifyResultToStatus`; LeanRetryResult (leanRetryLoop.ts:26) repeats the pattern with `unavailable?: boolean` optional. Nothing forces a consumer to call the interpreter — `verifyResultToStatus` takes a structural `{ valid; unavailable? }`, so the illegal state `{valid: true, unavailable: true}` is representable and formalizeNode compiles fine while ignoring the flag.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every present and future consumer of verifyLean/leanRetryLoop must independently remember to check `unavailable` before `valid`; forgetting is a silent wrong-verdict bug rather than a type error (already happened once, at formalizeNode.ts:61). Returning a single `VerificationStatus` (or a discriminated union `{status: \"valid\"} | {status: \"invalid\", errors} | {status: \"unavailable\", reason}`) would make the omission uncompilable and let `errors` be attached only to the arm that has them."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/components/ui/VerificationBadge.tsx",
    "line": 21,
    "summary": "The badge is an if-chain whose fallback is the red `Verification Failed`, so any VerificationStatus not explicitly listed renders as a failed proof. The new \"unavailable\" case had to be inserted before that fallback by hand. Contrast the sibling render sites NodeDetailPanel.tsx:23 (`STATUS_LABELS: Record<NodeVerificationStatus, …>`) and ProofGraphNode.tsx:6 (`STATUS_COLORS: Record<…>`), which are exhaustive Records that fail to compile when the union grows.",
    "failure_scenario": "The next status added to the union (e.g. \"stale\", \"timeout\") defaults to a red \"Verification Failed\" badge with no compiler warning — the same misleading-verdict class of bug this PR was written to fix. A `Record<VerificationStatus, {text, className, title}>` presentation map shared by badge, banner, dot and summary would have made this one edit instead of N leaf edits."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/hooks/usePanelDefinitions.tsx",
    "line": 110,
    "summary": "Two more status→presentation sites were not updated by this PR and swallow \"unavailable\" into a success-flavored default. The Lean panel `statusSummary` ternary handles only \"valid\"/\"invalid\", so unavailable falls to `\"Code ready\"`; SessionBanner.tsx:18 `statusDot` handles valid/invalid/verifying and gives unavailable the same neutral grey dot as \"none\".",
    "failure_scenario": "The panel rail reports \"Code ready\" and the session switcher shows a plain grey dot for a proof that was never checked, contradicting the amber warning the same PR adds two components away. A user scanning the session list cannot distinguish \"never verified\" from \"verifier was offline\", which is precisely the distinction the new status exists to make."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/api/verification/lean/route.ts",
    "line": 7,
    "summary": "`unavailableResponse` returns HTTP 200 with `unavailable: true` in the body, while the rest of the API layer signals upstream-service failure with HTTP 502 — see app/api/formalization/lean/route.ts:146, app/api/edit/{artifact,whole,inline}/route.ts, app/api/explanation/lean-error/route.ts:39 and the shared helper app/lib/formalization/artifactRoute.ts:110-124. The route also now discards the verifier's error body on `!res.ok` (previously forwarded with its status), so a genuine 4xx from the verifier (e.g. payload too large) is reported to the user as \"verifier offline\".",
    "failure_scenario": "Verification becomes the one route whose failures are invisible to any status-code-based layer — the shared `fetchApi` helper (api.ts:6) throws on non-OK, so a future refactor of verifyLean onto fetchApi would silently treat verifier-down as success; monitoring, proxies and Next.js error instrumentation all see 200. Conflating verifier 4xx with verifier-unreachable also hides real request-shape bugs behind an \"offline\" message that tells the user to set LEAN_VERIFIER_URL, which is already set."
  }
]
```

