    "failure_scenario": "With LEAN_VERIFIER_URL unset (the new default on any Vercel deploy, and now also local dev), a user formalizes a proposition node. leanRetryLoop returns unavailable on attempt 1, formalizeNode maps it to \"failed\", and the node turns red in ProofGraphNode (STATUS_COLORS.failed) with NodeDetailPanel showing \"Failed\" and an empty Verification Errors section. The user is told their proof was rejected by Lean when Lean was never run. This is exactly the false-negative the diff set out to prevent, but only the global pipeline path was fixed."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/hooks/useAutoFormalizeQueue.ts",
    "line": 136,
    "summary": "The queue consumes formalizeNode's \"verified\"|\"failed\" return. Because unavailable now maps to \"failed\" (see formalizeNode.ts:61), the queue does `failedIds.add(nodeId); failed++` and reports it in progress.failed. Worse, lines 112-120 then skip every downstream node whose `dependsOn` includes a failed id, marking them `verificationStatus: \"failed\"` with `\"Skipped: dependency <id> failed\"`. No branch distinguishes \"verifier offline\" from \"proof rejected\", so there is no way to make the queue stop/pause instead of burning the whole graph.",
    "failure_scenario": "User clicks auto-formalize on a 30-node decomposition while the verifier container is down. The first node's proof is generated fine but comes back unavailable -> \"failed\" -> added to failedIds. Every dependent node is then skipped without ever calling the LLM, and the run reports e.g. 1 verified / 29 failed. The user sees a fully red graph and concludes their paper's argument is broken, when nothing was ever checked. Restarting the verifier requires manually resetting 29 node statuses."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/types/decomposition.ts",
    "line": 36,
    "summary": "`toNodeVerificationStatus` has no case for the new \"unavailable\" member; it falls into `default: return \"unverified\"`. NodeVerificationStatus was not extended alongside VerificationStatus, so the round trip through `fromNodeVerificationStatus` (app/hooks/useActiveArtifactState.ts:42) turns \"unavailable\" into \"none\". The two call sites that lose the status are app/page.tsx:350 (nodePipeline.setVerificationStatus) and app/page.tsx:186 (handleRestoreSession).",
    "failure_scenario": "In decomposition mode with a node selected, the user hits Verify. useFormalizationPipeline correctly computes vStatus = \"unavailable\", but page.tsx:350 funnels it through toNodeVerificationStatus -> \"unverified\", and useActiveArtifactState maps it back to \"none\" for the panels. VerificationBadge returns null (status === \"none\"), LeanCodeDisplay's new amber \"Verifier offline — proof not checked\" banner never renders, and the new Re-verify button never appears. The entire feature added by this diff is silently a no-op for node-scoped sessions — the user sees Lean code with no badge at all and no indication it was never checked."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/api/verification/lean/route.ts",
    "line": 26,
    "summary": "The diff deleted the `?? \"http://localhost:3100\"` fallback for LEAN_VERIFIER_URL. Nothing in the repo sets that variable: there is no .env / .env.example, and docker-compose.yml does not inject it into the Next.js process. README.md:84 still states \"defaults to http://localhost:3100\" and README.md:92 / docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:204,234 still document the removed `{valid:true, mock:true}` fallback.",
    "failure_scenario": "A developer follows the documented workflow — `docker compose up` starts the real Lean verifier on port 3100, then `npm run dev`. Because LEAN_VERIFIER_URL is unset, the route short-circuits at line 27 and returns `unavailable: \"verifier-not-configured\"` for every request. The running verifier is never contacted, so real Lean type-checking is dead in local development and in CI, and every proof shows \"Verifier offline\" — a silent regression that the docs actively mislead the developer away from diagnosing."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/api/verification/lean/route.ts",
    "line": 45,
    "summary": "The non-OK branch changed from `NextResponse.json(data, { status: res.status })` (forwarding the verifier's own body) to `unavailableResponse(\"verifier-error\", \"HTTP ${res.status}\")`, and the `await res.json()` was moved after the check so the error body is never parsed. Any non-2xx the verifier uses to signal a *checked but rejected* proof — or to report the specific Lean error payload — is now discarded and reported as \"the verifier is offline\".",
    "failure_scenario": "The Lean verifier returns 400/422 with `{valid:false, errors:\"unknown identifier 'foo'\"}` for a genuinely malformed proof. The route now swallows the body and responds `{valid:false, unavailable:true, reason:\"verifier-error\", detail:\"HTTP 422\"}`. verifyResultToStatus yields \"unavailable\", leanRetryLoop returns early with `errors: \"\"` and skips all remaining repair attempts, and the UI shows the amber \"Verifier offline — proof not checked\" banner. The user loses the actual Lean error message and the self-repair loop, and is told the service is down when it answered correctly."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/hooks/usePanelDefinitions.tsx",
    "line": 110,
    "summary": "The Lean panel's `statusSummary` ternary chain handles only \"valid\" and \"invalid\" and falls through to `activeLeanCode ? \"Code ready\" : \"No code yet\"`; \"unavailable\" is not handled. The same omission exists in app/components/features/session-banner/SessionBanner.tsx:18-23, where `statusDot` special-cases valid/invalid/verifying and returns the neutral grey dot (`bg-[#9A9590]`, identical to \"none\") for \"unavailable\".",
    "failure_scenario": "After a verification attempt against an offline verifier, the panel sidebar reads \"Lean4 Code — Code ready\" while the panel header simultaneously shows the amber \"Verifier offline — not checked\" badge, and the session switcher shows the same grey dot as a session that was never formalized. A user scanning the sidebar or session list cannot distinguish an unchecked proof from a fresh/idle one, and may export or trust the Lean code believing it was verified."
  }
]
```

