    "notes": "Verified: `clearTimeout(timeout)` (line 43) only runs when fetch resolves; the catch (lines 53-56) returns without clearing, and there is no finally. On fetch rejection (ECONNREFUSED/DNS/abort) the 35s timer stays pending and later fires `controller.abort()` on an already-settled request. However, two mitigating facts: (1) this is PRE-EXISTING — `git show main:app/api/verification/lean/route.ts` has the identical setTimeout/clearTimeout-after-await/catch-without-clear structure, so the diff did not introduce it (the diff did rewrite the surrounding function, so it counts as touched code and is reportable, but should be framed as pre-existing, not a regression). (2) Severity is low: the leak is bounded — each failed request retains one timer + AbortController for at most 35s, then the abort() is a no-op on the settled fetch; nothing accumulates beyond that window and no request hangs. The finding's mechanics and the try/finally fix are correct; its implicit framing that the diff created the leak is not. Also note the diff arguably makes it slightly LESS likely in one config: main defaulted to http://localhost:3100 (guaranteed ECONNREFUSED when unconfigured), whereas HEAD returns early at line 29 before ever creating the timer when LEAN_VERIFIER_URL is unset — the reject path now requires a configured-but-wrong/down URL."
  },
  {
    "finding": 2,
    "verdict": "REFUTED",
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/formalization/leanRetryLoop.ts",
    "line": 75,
    "notes": "The scenario is reachable (attempt 1 invalid -> onErrors(E1) -> attempt 2 regenerates code -> verify returns unavailable -> onErrors(\"\")), but the wipe is correct, deliberate, and consistent, not a bug. Key facts: (1) Before the unavailable verify on attempt 2, `onLeanCode(currentCode)` (line 62) has already replaced the displayed/persisted code with the regenerated v2 — E1 describes superseded v1 code that no longer exists in the UI or session. Keeping E1 would misattribute old diagnostics to new code. (2) This exactly mirrors the valid path (lines 79-81: onErrors(\"\"), errors:\"\"), while the exhausted-attempts path (line 88) returns lastErrors precisely because those errors DO correspond to the final currentCode — the asymmetry the finding highlights is principled, not accidental. (3) The consumer explicitly documents this as design: useFormalizationPipeline.ts lines 121-126 — comment 'Only \"invalid\" carries verifier output; \"valid\"/\"unavailable\" clear errors.' — and independently clears errors for any non-invalid status, so even if leanRetryLoop returned E1 the hook would discard it. verifyWithDeps (lines 143-147) does the same. (4) The user is not left uninformed: `unavailable: true` maps to verificationStatus \"unavailable\" (api.ts verifyResultToStatus, line 115-116), which VerificationBadge.tsx and LeanCodeDisplay.tsx render as a distinct 'never checked' state persisted to the session. (5) In the most common unavailable case (verifier down from attempt 1) there are no prior errors to wipe at all. At most this is a debatable UX preference (retaining historical attempt-1 diagnostics), not destroyed state the design intended to keep."
  }
